elaine: Heads up 1: I've been iterating on the 'admission token economy' idea from the autoscaler RFC. Prototype publishes fractional admission tokens + per-SKU efficiency instead of raw GPU counts. want feedback on eviction semantics and operator UX.

mike: What problem does this solve most? packing efficiency, or decoupling cost from capacity?

elaine: both. packing small/short requests wastes less prewarm with tokens. also lets routing use cost_estimate without embedding raw capacity. proto sketch below.

elaine: ```
message TokenHeadroom {
  int64 ts = 1;
  int64 seq = 2; // monotonic snapshot id
  double cost_usd_hr = 3;
  repeated SkuFactor sku = 4;
  repeated TokenBalance balances = 5;
}
message SkuFactor {
  string sku_name = 1;
  double effective_gpu = 2; // 1.0 for A100, 0.35 for A10
}
message TokenBalance {
  string tenant = 1;
  map<string,double> tokens_by_kind = 2; // {"gpu":0.5, "kv":0.2}
}
```

raj: Concern: eviction and preemption currently use discrete slots and tenant priority. fractional tokens could confuse the eviction comparator.

elaine: Proposal: tokens are advisory for admission and soft-eviction scoring only. Hard eviction decisions still consult reserved_slots and active_lease metadata. Scorer example:

elaine: eviction_score = base_priority + penalty_preemptible - gamma * min(token_balance_effective, cap) + delta * cost_spike

Sophia: small nit: cap to token influence so a tenant with tiny balance doesn't immediately become top eviction target. also add ttl on snapshots. stale tokens must not flip decisions.

mike: Agree. Add seq + ttl. If snapshot older than TTL, fallback to operator absolute capacity publish. Also record token_staleness metric and have the scorer ignore token field when staleness > threshold.

carol: Operators will ask: how do we show this in the UI? Need clear mapping: "0.1 token ~= one short request" and per-sku normalization visible. Add debug verbose snapshots for /debug=true.

yana: Billing interplay: cost_usd_hr propagated in snapshot could be used by routing and billing teams. make sure it's read-only for billing to avoid dual counting during failovers. single authority rule.

elaine: rollout plan: 1) implement proto with seq+ttl 2) implement sku_factor table and conversion 3) scorer impl with capped token impact 4) canary on canary-3 for 1 hour with eviction_churn and tail-latency monitors 5) rollback if eviction_churn > 3x or p95 tail > 1.2x baseline.

mike: Test matrix: mostly-small (80% short), mixed-large (30% large), burst-prewarm test. Measure p50/p95 tail, eviction_count, token_staleness histogram, and billing-reads-per-minute.

raj: One more operational concern: mixed-SKU packing. If token balances normalize to effective-gpu, admission must ensure we don't pack across incompatible SKUs (e.g., A100-only model). Need an admission filter for sku_compatible flag.

elaine: yes add tokens_by_kind with kind metadata and require admission check sku_compatible. also add "reserved_token_flag" on balances to protect hard SLAs from token depletion.

autoscaler-bot: Canary run queued: headroom-token-economy on canary-3 at 2026-03-23T15:00Z.

carol: ops note: when you publish tokens, also publish a sample handful of expanded debug entries every minute so SRE can inspect real mapping without toggling debug for everyone.

elaine: Ack. I'll include that in follow-up RFC patch. Also adding a short example mapping in the doc: 0.05 token ~= single-chat-completion short request; 0.5 token ~= long-chat or 5120-token body.

mike: :thumbsup: we should be conservative for first rollout. keep hard SLA shield & seq/ttl. LGTM for canary once RFC link is posted.

elaine: I'll open the RFC follow-up with proto diff, scorer pseudo-code, test plan, dashboard links, and rollback steps. tagging @mike @sophia @raj @carol @yana for review.

autoscaler-bot: Canary metrics posted: first 15m window: token_staleness_med=1.1s, eviction_churn=0.9/10m, p95_tail +2.8% vs baseline. Dashboard: https://dashboards.redwood.internal/d/xyz/headroom-token-econ

elaine: nice. closing this thread, will ping for final review on RFC. DM me for urgent issues.
